    "summary": "This 1933 Packard Twelve is a Series 1005 seven-passenger touring car that was delivered to its first owner in March 1933 and is said to have remained with its second owner into the 1970s. The car underwent a refurbishment in the 1980s and was subsequently sold in 1984 to an owner who retained it for 25 years. It then spent a decade in the collection of the Academy of Art University Car Museum in San Francisco before being purchased by its previous owner, who commissioned a refresh that included the installation of a replacement dark blue convertible top. The car was then purchased by the seller in 2021 and has since remained part of a collection stored in a gymnasium. Power is provided by a 445.5ci L-head V12 that breathes through a dual-throat Stromberg carburetor and is mated to a floor-shifted three-speed manual transmission with free-wheeling. Finished in beige and dark blue, the car also features mechanically actuated drum brakes with variable vacuum assistance, adjustable ride-control suspension, 17” blue-painted wire wheels, dual side-mount spares, a fold-down rear luggage rack, dark blue tufted leather interior upholstery, and fold-down middle-row jump seats. This Packard Twelve touring is now offered with Vermont registration.",
